{"id":"cable_pull_through","name":"Cable pull-through","cue":"Hips back, back flat; squeeze your glutes to stand tall.","research":"Cable pull-through at a low pulley (wheel 0.1 H = 18 cm high, 0.42 H = 0.75 m behind the mid-sole line, the column 0.09 H further back, the carriage at its lowest hole): the lifter stands with his back to the column, feet 0.15 H each side of the midline (53 cm apart, toes out 15 deg) on a floor plate, holding the two ends of a rope in a neutral grip (palms facing, fists 0.044 H apart); the two strands (0.17 H from the fist to the ring) run back between the thighs to a ring on the cable. Design values from the standard technique description, not a measured trial: a hip hinge with a neutral spine (spinal flexion 7 at the bottom, 1 at the top), trunk 66 deg from vertical and hip flexion 82 at the bottom, upright with hip flexion 6 at the top, knees soft (29 at the bottom, 10 at the top), shins near vertical (ankle 3), the hips travelling back about 0.05 H. The arms stay long with a soft elbow (14 deg) and the hands rise in a near-vertical line in front of the knees and thighs: 0.29 H high and 0.10 H ahead of the mid-sole line at the bottom (shoulder flexion 56, the arms 6 deg behind the vertical, the wrist 13 deg radial so the fist tunnel follows the rope), 0.48 H high and 0.095 H ahead at the top (shoulder flexion 21), never closer than 0.027 H (5 cm) to the legs. The ring stays between the thighs 0.037 H (6.6 cm) or more clear of the skin and the strands 0.013 H (2.3 cm) or more; the cable runs from the ring to the pulley at every phase and the stack rises 0.094 H (17 cm) as the lifter stands. Primary: gluteus maximus and both hamstring heads; secondary: the erector spinae (label Lower back), held isometrically. Geometry: design/fix/pt.mjs.","targets":{"tol":10,"tolBy":{"wrist_deviation":10,"hip_abduction":10},"note":"design values (standard technique), not a measured trial; elbow, shoulder and wrist are the hand placement, the ankle fol